{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Yenisoy2019-MWIR-AR-Si-3L",
  "Source": "A. Yenisoy, C. Yesilyaprak, and S. Tuzemen, \"High efficient ultra-broadband anti-reflection coating on silicon for infrared applications,\" Infrared Phys. Technol. 100, 82-86 (2019). doi:10.1016/j.infrared.2019.05.014 - Table 1, p. 84 (layer #, thickness nm, QWOT at 550 nm, material); dispersions Eq. (3)-(4), p. 84-85",
  "Notes": "Broadband MWIR (3.4-4.8 um) antireflection coating on silicon: Al2O3 (nL) / Ge (nH) / Al2O3 (nL)\nSpecification: Target Rav = 0 with Rav < 0.5 % acceptance and Tav = 100 % with Tav > 99.5 % acceptance over 3.4-4.8 um, AOI 0 deg, unpolarized (p. 85).\nReported: Table 2 (p. 85), 3.4-4.8 um: simulated Rav = 0.212 %, Tav = 99.788 %; actual (measured) Rav = 0.214 %, Tav = 99.786 %. Fig. 7 simulated R: ~0.08 % minimum near 3.5 um, ~0.22 % plateau 3.8-4.4 um, rising to ~0.45 % at 4.8 um; simulated T ~99.93 % peak near 3.5 um, ~99.78 % at 4.0 um, ~99.8 % at 4.4 um, falling to ~99.55 % at 4.8 um. Design total physical thickness = 925.49 nm (sum of Table 1, not printed); measured total 915 nm; random error analysis (Fig. 6) gives errors -3, -1, -6.5 nm on layers 1, 2, 3 (Table 1 numbering, from the substrate), total 914 nm. QWOTs printed in Table 1 at 550 nm: 1.6 (134.969 nm L), 3.19 (85 nm H), 8.36 (705.521 nm L). My check with these dispersions (one coated surface, semi-infinite Si, normal incidence) gives Rav ~0.209 % over 3.4-4.8 um (R 0.108 % at 3.4, 0.081 % at 3.5, 0.249 % at 4.0, 0.201 % at 4.4, 0.380 % at 4.8 um), matching the reported 0.212 %.\nNotes: Table 1 numbers the layers from the substrate (OptiLayer convention). Recomputation confirmed this: with the 705.521 nm Al2O3 next to air the band-average R is ~0.21 % as reported, while the reverse order gives R of tens of percent. The list here is Table 1 reversed. Thicknesses are used in nm. The Table 1 QWOT column at the 550 nm monitor wavelength is not reproduced by the IR dispersion formulas extrapolated to 550 nm (they give Al2O3 n ~1.606 -> QWOT 1.58, and Ge n ~8.5 -> 5.3); the QWOT column implies other visible indices (n ~1.63 for Al2O3, ~5.16 for Ge), so it should not be used to build the stack. The coating was applied on both sides of the substrate in the experiment, but the Table 2/Fig. 7 values behave as single-surface values (semi-infinite Si), which is how the stack is built here; substrate thickness is not given. Films treated as non-absorbing (paper: T + R = 100 % for single films). Design AOI 0 deg; FTIR measurement at 10 deg AOI.\nLayers from Table 1, p. 84 (layer #, thickness nm, QWOT at 550 nm, material); dispersions Eq. (3)-(4), p. 84-85; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours Rav 0.209 %, Tav 99.791 % over 3.4-4.8 um; paper Rav 0.212 %, Tav 99.788 % (simulated).",
